WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.760
- [Mark] All right, Adam.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:04.890
Adam, where'd you grow up?

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.440
Where are you from originally?

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.683
- Los Angeles, California,
born and raised.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.170
Spent some of my younger
years in Northern California

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:15.223
in Sonoma County.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.880
When my birth parents split up,

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.150
then end up moving back to Los Angeles

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.910
and spent most of my time
growing up here in LA.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.863
- [Mark] And you, tell
me about your childhood,

00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:27.430
you had both parents?

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:29.910
- Well, my father stayed
in Northern California

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:34.590
and my mother and I
moved with my stepfather,

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:35.963
who is George DiCaprio.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.410
My mother met George in San Francisco.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.080
She was in a guerrilla
theater dance troupe

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.080
called Freestore that was led
by a man named Vito Paulekas

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.560
who, at one point in the late '60s,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.340
was part of the love-in movement

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:57.320
and the drop-in, get high,
the whole '60s thing,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.120
in Laurel Canyon with
The Mothers of Invention

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.302
and Frank Zappa.

00:01:00.302 --> 00:01:01.384
- [Mark] Right.

00:01:01.384 --> 00:01:04.640
- And they started the first
love-ins in Griffith Park,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.640
and this guy Vito was
a sculpting instructor

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.290
at Barnsdall Park, actually, in Hollywood,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.970
and in the late '60s, early
'70s, a lot of the hippies

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.600
from that movement moved
to Northern California

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.980
to Sonoma County.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:20.260
Vito was one of them and he started

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.740
this sort of vaudevillian
theater dance troupe

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:24.740
that my mom was a part of,

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.670
and they did these really elaborate acts

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.020
that were costumed and
choreographed and everything,

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.360
and they would perform
on stage in Berkeley

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.510
and San Francisco and Union
Square and these public places,

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:42.270
and they were sort of
like political satire,

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.930
anti-establishment, like
skits and stuff like that.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.068
There was one actually where
my mom assassinated Santa Claus

00:01:49.068 --> 00:01:51.916
(chuckles) in fishnet
stockings and high heels.

00:01:51.916 --> 00:01:52.749
- [Mark] Fun.

00:01:52.749 --> 00:01:56.720
- And it was pretty cool, and
so it was called Freestore.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.840
The Freestore was actually
a shack that Vito had built

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.340
in this little town in
Sonoma County called Cotati

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.820
where it was like a Goodwill.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.050
It was like a homemade Goodwill,
so it had a sign on the top

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.800
that said Freestore and people
would go and drop shit off

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.110
and you could go and pick things up,

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.250
clothes and toys and stuff like that.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.100
So, anyway, my mom was a
part of this dance troupe.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.890
She met George, they hit it off.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:19.660
We moved back to Los Angeles,

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:24.660
and shortly thereafter,
after coming back to LA,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.960
I started to work on television.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.480
I got work on TV commercials.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.300
There was a girl in my first grade class

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.300
whose mom was a manager, and
she met my mom at a PTA meeting

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.530
and told my mom that I was
gregarious and outspoken

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.020
and articulate for my age,
and that I would do good

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.630
to go see this agent.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:44.750
- [Mark] As an actor.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.400
- Yeah, in Sunset Plaza,
this agent, Iris Burton,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.210
to go see her and see
if she would sign me,

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:50.760
go out on auditions, and she did,

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.410
she signed me immediately
and I started going out

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.870
on additions and making good money.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:58.420
So we grew up in Los
Feliz, right in Los Feliz,

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:00.960
east of Hollywood, in a little house,

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.330
in a little white Craftsman house,

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:06.430
and Leo lived with his mom one block over,

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.200
kind of our houses were separated.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.540
The backyards were separated
by a dirt lot, this vacant lot.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:12.670
All they did in the winters

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.680
was they sold Christmas trees there.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.080
- [Mark] So your stepbrother is Leonardo.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.270
- Is Leonardo di Caprio, yeah.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:18.300
- [Mark] Got it, okay.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.960
- Yeah, yeah, so when I was four,

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.030
that's about four, between four and five,

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.530
that's when we moved back to
LA, and my mom met his dad,

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.450
and they've been married ever since.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.200
Never had any more kids,
never had any other siblings

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.890
or anything besides he and I.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.481
Then my dad remarried and he
lived in Northern California

00:03:34.481 --> 00:03:37.610
and I would spend summers,
I would, summer vacations,

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.440
Christmas vacations, I would leave LA.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.250
I'd get out of LA, I'd go
to Northern California,

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.880
I'd spend with my dad, so
I had this really magical,

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.730
fairytale-like time in
my summer vacations,

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.409
hanging out in Sonoma County.

00:03:49.409 --> 00:03:50.420
- [Mark] Yeah, Sonoma's beautiful.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:54.200
- In the 80s, with BMX bikes
and smoking really good pot

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.840
and hanging out, being a teenager,

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.660
swimming in the rushing river
and the creeks and stuff

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.210
when the water was still clean,

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:00.720
when you didn't get ear
infections and stuff

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:01.690
swimming in swimming holes

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:04.280
and you could actually swim in rivers

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.180
and the creeks and stuff when I was a kid,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.000
and yeah, it was a really special time.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.720
Then my grandmother
moved, my dad lived in LA.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.130
See, everybody grew up
in LA and then some moved

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:16.520
out to Northern California.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.940
My grandmother on my father's
side lived in Mendocino,

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.390
the town of Mendocino,
and that was beautiful.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:22.600
I'd spend Christmases up there.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.140
Every Christmas, we'd go
to the town of Mendocino,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:25.973
and my grandmother lived

00:04:25.973 --> 00:04:28.580
in this 135-year-old Victorian house,

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:31.690
this real old fucking cool
house up there on the headlands

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:34.820
in this historic town
in Northern California,

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.530
so, those were my Christmases.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:37.860
- [Mark] So, it sounds
like a great childhood.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.790
- Yeah, it was cool
growing up, so it was both.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.130
I got the dichotomy of
living in Los Angeles

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:44.053
and experiencing that.

00:04:45.074 --> 00:04:48.313
I kind of burned out on
acting when I was young.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.790
It was a lot, it was get out
of school and go on auditions,

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:56.250
and that was every day, so,
as a teenager, 15, I remember,

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:59.377
around 14, 15, I started burning out

00:04:59.377 --> 00:05:00.210
and I got into skateboarding

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:03.370
and I kind of went to
that instead, and Leo,

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:06.670
my little brother, he saw me doing that,

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:07.503
saw me making money.

00:05:07.503 --> 00:05:09.193
We didn't spend any of the money.

00:05:09.193 --> 00:05:12.515
This is a little caveat is
all of the money that I made

00:05:12.515 --> 00:05:15.580
when I was acting, since
we were on food stamps

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:19.230
and welfare growing up, all
of that money got put away

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.560
as a ward of the court and
the judge oversaw the account,

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.990
so any money was gonna be done
anything with that account,

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.260
a judge had to oversee
it and have a court order

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:27.750
to do anything with that money.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.970
So when I was 18, I got
handed all of the money

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.190
that I made acting.

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:34.870
It was like $186,000, something like that.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.380
I had all that money when I was 18.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:38.380
- [Mark] How old are you now?

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.600
- I'm 48 now, so, there's a big gap there.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.030
So, 18, so got handed that money.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.950
Actually partied, had a really good time

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:49.090
with some of the money for a little bit

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:51.180
then tried to go to college,
it didn't really work out,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:52.490
joined the army.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:55.061
Went off to the army for four years.

00:05:55.061 --> 00:05:57.000
Did the army for four years, did two tours

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.203
in the first Gulf War.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.220
Desert Shield, Desert Storm,

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:05.073
stationed at Abu Dhabi Air
Force Base in Saudi Arabia.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.140
So did two tours in the first Gulf War,

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:10.890
did four years at Fort Benning, Georgia,

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:13.900
and got out of the army, went to college,

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.640
went to Pasadena City
College for two years,

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.650
and then transferred to USC,

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:21.330
and went there for two years.

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:23.670
Got my Bachelor of Arts at USC.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.330
And then for a little
while, for some time,

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:26.520
worked for my brother.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.130
Right around this time
was when Titanic came out.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:32.123
So I actually started a
company that had to do with,

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:36.050
it's called Fan Effects, and
we were gonna do fan sections

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:38.140
of websites and official merchandising

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.890
and everything like that for celebrities,

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:44.900
and I started that company
based on his success in Titanic.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:46.150
Company actually did really well.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:48.600
We made a million
dollars in the first year

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.290
and I ended up just
selling off the company

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:53.000
and taking an $80,000 severance pay,

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:56.590
something like that,
so, that was in the 90s.

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:58.920
In the 90s, I started
partying a lot in Hollywood,

00:06:58.920 --> 00:06:59.940
clubs and stuff in Hollywood,

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.617
doing a lot of drugs and stuff,

00:07:02.562 --> 00:07:05.619
so, that's kind of progressed
as the years have gone by.

00:07:05.619 --> 00:07:06.452
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:07:06.452 --> 00:07:09.903
When did drugs really get
into the picture for you?

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:12.120
- Well, they were always in the picture

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:14.680
but it was always just
sort of pot growing up.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:18.787
It was always pot, and I'd
say I guess in the 90s,

00:07:18.787 --> 00:07:22.830
after '95, '96, around those years,

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:25.640
just hanging out more in clubs
or in Hollywood drinking,

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.290
doing coke and stuff like
that, staying up all night,

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:32.350
but really, no serious, regular addictions

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:33.183
or anything like that.

00:07:33.183 --> 00:07:35.550
More sort of just partying
and binge drinking

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:36.630
and stuff like that.

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:40.380
Opiates and real addiction
and stuff like that,

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:44.253
for me, really took over more,
I'd say in the last 10 years,

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.880
something like that,
yeah, since 2008, 2009,

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.847
somewhere around there.

00:07:49.847 --> 00:07:50.680
- [Mark] And was it--

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:51.513
- Started kinda late.

00:07:51.513 --> 00:07:54.380
- [Mark] Was, had you been
playing around with heroin

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:56.660
before that or is it?

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:59.160
- Yeah, I had used socially.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.390
I'd been able to use
casually here and there.

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:06.270
I used cocaine, ecstasy, drank, of course,

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:09.910
and pot, there's always pot,
and a little bit of meth.

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:13.890
There was really good,
called it glass back then,

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:17.730
it was glass, but never
really had one drug

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.320
that really got me, that
I spent every day going

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:22.230
and getting money and buying and scoring

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:25.160
like I am now with
heroin, or fentanyl now,

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:26.760
but, now it's switched over to fentanyl.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:27.810
Now it's not heroin anymore.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:31.690
Now we all do fentanyl now,
which is strange, but...

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:33.270
- [Mark] You have to be
careful with that, right?

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:34.550
- Yeah, yeah, you do.

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:36.991
For the most part, yeah,
you have to be careful,

00:08:36.991 --> 00:08:38.090
so, regulate how much we use.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:39.520
These small amounts and stuff,

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.110
but you still have to be careful.

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:44.140
I always have Narcan on hand, so.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:45.290
- [Mark] Have you OD'd?

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:49.890
- I OD'd one time, yeah.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:51.240
Once I OD'd.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.720
I had been in jail for
about eight or nine days.

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.670
This was when I was still
doing heroin, and I did a shot,

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:01.670
I did a one, a 1., so .10 amount
of black heroin in a shot,

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:06.580
and I went out, I planked out,

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:09.670
and I fell down and I
seized up a little bit,

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:11.990
hit my head kinda on the
bathroom counter on the way down,

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:15.000
and they gave me one dose of Narcan,

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.860
and that one does of
Narcan brought me back.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.660
10 minutes later, I was fine, I was awake

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:23.660
and I felt fine because if
your system is cleaned out

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.750
and you're truly ODing
and you get Narcanned,

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.320
you don't go into this
horrible forced withdrawal.

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:30.730
If you have a bunch of dope in your system

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:35.320
and you get Narcanned, you go
into this horrible withdrawal,

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:36.380
but I didn't get that.

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:37.643
- [Mark] And have you been clean?

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:40.820
- I've been working on getting clean.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.470
I've been back and forth,
back and forth with it

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:45.260
for some time now, so struggling with it.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:47.530
I want to get back into the
good graces of my family,

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:49.640
and Charity, my wife and I

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:51.500
have a 12-year-old daughter together,

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:53.280
who's living now with my mom,

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:55.160
and I want to have her back in my life,

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:58.710
so I've been working, trying to get,

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:01.120
aside from the place that
we have, we rent a room

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:02.710
but I'm trying to get
my own house going on,

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:04.350
going through that whole process,

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:06.330
through housing and social
security and all that,

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:08.063
and getting those services set up.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:12.870
I am service rated disabled
through the VA also.

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:14.250
I get disability through the VA

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:16.260
and I'm working on getting SSI.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.948
So I already have benefits through VA

00:10:18.948 --> 00:10:21.270
and I'm getting that
monthly currently already,

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:24.420
so, I'm well on my way
to getting things set up

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:26.130
and getting my ducks in order.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:27.530
It's taken some time, but...

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:32.584
- [Mark] What does fentanyl do for you?

00:10:32.584 --> 00:10:34.030
- It's similar to heroin.

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:36.050
I mean, it basically replaced
the heroin addiction.

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:37.920
We started doing heroin that
had a lot of fentanyl in it.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:39.550
- [Mark] Is it basically the same?

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:41.170
- They sort, pretty much the same,

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:43.740
the same high, the same
kind of get down, the same,

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.840
you have to keep it in your
system or you're gonna get sick.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:47.800
You have to stay ahead of the addiction,

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:50.390
and basically just a way to check out,

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:54.190
basically a way to kinda just
stay numb for the most part.

00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:56.050
- [Mark] I mean, do you,
I mean, you don't sound

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:57.750
like you have some childhood events

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.870
that you're trying to numb out.

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:01.530
- No, not particularly, no.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:03.210
More just sort of the pain of, I mean,

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.590
I do have the separation from my family,

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:08.323
and I don't really have
that connection and that,

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:12.449
I don't really have that
closeness with my family,

00:11:12.449 --> 00:11:14.420
any of my family members,
except for my father.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:16.070
He's all the way at, my
father's in Hawaii now,

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:21.070
my blood father, but yeah,
they sort of have distanced me

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:24.837
and black sheeped and
pariahed me, you know?

00:11:25.881 --> 00:11:26.714
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:11:26.714 --> 00:11:27.547
- Marginalized me.

00:11:27.547 --> 00:11:28.380
- [Mark] You're a drug addict.

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:29.920
- As it were, so yeah, yeah.

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:31.460
- [Mark] You're a dreaded drug addict.

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:35.075
- Yeah, the family drug
addict, so, there's always one.

00:11:35.075 --> 00:11:36.442
That's me.

00:11:36.442 --> 00:11:38.109
- [Mark] Yeah, so...

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:40.740
- I don't mean to
trivialize it or anything.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:43.880
It's sad, it is what it is.

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:45.990
It's my choice, I take
responsibility for it,

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:49.870
and I don't really point
the finger, like you said,

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:54.080
at any childhood event or
any abuse or molestation

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:56.423
or anything like that, I can't really,

00:11:56.423 --> 00:12:01.423
I mean, for the most part, our
parents provided for us well.

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:03.280
Even though we were on
food stamps and welfare,

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:06.990
they still took us to the
movies and amusement parks

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:10.870
and family outings on weekends
and then hiking in the hills

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:12.480
and lots of stuff.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:16.290
Tried to, cultured us, the
parades and different events

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:19.210
and social events and
different cultural events

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:20.770
around the city growing up.

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:24.810
Museums and, there's a
lot to experience in LA.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.010
There used to be this big
thing called the Street Scene

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:27.860
in Downtown LA.

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:28.693
It was really cool.

00:12:28.693 --> 00:12:30.731
It was, streets were all blocked off,

00:12:30.731 --> 00:12:31.913
there were concerts and stuff,

00:12:31.913 --> 00:12:33.700
and that was all the way up till the 80s.

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:35.011
- [Mark] Is there anything that,

00:12:35.011 --> 00:12:37.670
like having a wildly successful brother?

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:41.080
- That definitely, at one
point, when I stopped working

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:43.430
for the Fan Effects, the company
that I made out of college,

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:45.740
when I kind of went off
and did my own thing,

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:48.200
there was a little bit of a turning away,

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:52.220
a little bit of a pity
party sort of a thing.

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:54.875
- It's kind of like having
a wildly successful father.

00:12:54.875 --> 00:12:56.290
- Right, and then you
get your own way and go,

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:58.280
screw this, I don't need this.

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:00.890
It's the ultimate, fuck
you, I don't need this,

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:01.863
you know what I mean?

00:13:01.863 --> 00:13:03.600
I don't want to be a part of
this and then walking away,

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:05.225
and then hoping that somebody comes

00:13:05.225 --> 00:13:08.690
and invites you anyway to the premiere.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:11.450
I have, all the way up until recently,

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:12.670
I was doing pretty well.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:16.970
I was working in television production.

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:19.960
I'm actually Local 44
in the Carpenters' Union

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:24.700
for TV commercials, music
videos, so I work on sets.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.630
I'm a set decorator and a carpenter

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:29.440
and a builder and a prop guy.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:34.021
I do all that stuff, and I've
been doing that since 2002.

00:13:34.021 --> 00:13:35.765
That's when I worked on my first video.

00:13:35.765 --> 00:13:38.690
We worked on 50 Cent's
first video, Wanksta.

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:41.095
Filmed it right over here
on 8th and Normandie,

00:13:41.095 --> 00:13:42.500
(scoffs) and that's how I kind of broke

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:43.700
into that whole industry

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:46.900
was working on low-budget
hip hop videos at first.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:48.860
Guys that were just
getting into the industry,

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:51.540
and then, actually, as 50 Cent took off,

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:53.710
as his career took off,
so did a bunch of us

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:56.060
who were working in that
business at the time.

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:59.360
We were able to kind of
piggyback off of his success

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:02.240
and move up in that world,
which was kind of cool.

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:03.467
So I've done that for quite some time

00:14:03.467 --> 00:14:04.680
and I could go back to that

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:08.110
and my family would take
me back in probably,

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:10.483
and probably have a trust
fund and all of that,

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:14.460
but I just have to stay clean.

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:17.320
The main thing is they just,
they want me to be clean.

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:20.440
My mom kinda got wind that
I was hooked on heroin

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:23.050
and then I was kind of
just spinning my wheels

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:24.130
and not really moving forward

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:26.840
and she made a couple of
phone calls and CPS came.

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:30.320
She was able to get custody of
our daughter, which is okay.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:33.270
She's taken good care of,
my parents live right over,

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:36.040
not far from us in Silver
Lake, and they got a big house

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:37.910
with the pool and a screening room,

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:40.470
so she's able to have friends
over and have movie night

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:42.400
and pool parties and
everything, so, she's--

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.290
- [Mark] And how old's your daughter?

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:45.262
- She's 12 years old now.

00:14:45.262 --> 00:14:46.239
- [Mark] 12, and does she know you--

00:14:46.239 --> 00:14:47.072
- She lived with us all the way up

00:14:47.072 --> 00:14:48.773
until four or five years ago,
until my mom got custody.

00:14:48.773 --> 00:14:50.830
- [Mark] Does she know that
you guys are down here?

00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:52.180
- She doesn't know we're downtown,

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:53.440
she doesn't know specifically.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.910
We don't talk specifically all the time.

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:57.860
Her mother has more
contact with her than I do.

00:14:57.860 --> 00:14:59.040
- [Mark] Then you, but
you visit her often?

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:02.650
- So, no, we've stopped doing visitation.

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:05.650
We're at the point now where,
it's been a long, hard battle.

00:15:05.650 --> 00:15:10.650
We've burned through 15,
close to $20,000 in attorneys

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:13.050
that haven't been able to do anything.

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:14.557
They just kind of take your money.

00:15:14.557 --> 00:15:16.963
$20,000, they just kind of laugh at that.

00:15:16.963 --> 00:15:19.430
That's just kind of like an
ante up kind of an amount,

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:23.550
and so we've gone through
hell with court battles

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:26.253
and custody courts and stuff like that,

00:15:27.106 --> 00:15:27.939
and we're still working on it

00:15:27.939 --> 00:15:29.340
but we're kind of defeated right now.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:33.460
We've kind of been, we feel a
little bit conspired against

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:38.460
by the system and by the, by
what seems to be some type

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:41.440
of corruption within the
system and the ability

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:46.440
of our parents to manipulate
the powers that be

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:49.250
to get what they want, basically.

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:52.620
So, it's just kind of
defeating and deflating

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:56.583
and energy-draining, to say the least, so.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:00.293
- [Mark] Yeah, it must be.

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:03.650
But so sometimes you're
staying here on Skid Row.

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:06.650
- Yeah, yeah, sometimes, yeah,
sometimes for days at a time,

00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:08.120
we'll be down here.

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:09.440
We do have a place in
Silver Lake where we go,

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:13.180
where we stay sometimes,
too, but just because of lack

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:14.750
of having enough drugs or whatever,

00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:16.150
sometimes we'll just stay downtown.

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:18.320
- [Mark] And you're staying in a tent or?

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:20.130
- I have stayed in tents.

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:22.440
We do have various places
where we stay indoors.

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:24.330
We have rented rooms in different places

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:26.280
and have friends that have SROs

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:27.680
and when it gets really cold,

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:29.926
you kind of try to figure
out where to go indoors.

00:16:29.926 --> 00:16:32.350
- [Mark] I mean, do you have
still have money saved or?

00:16:32.350 --> 00:16:33.540
- No, but I do get monthly.

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.330
I get money every month from
the VA for my disability still,

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:37.630
so I do have income.

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:38.724
- [Mark] Got it.

00:16:38.724 --> 00:16:41.115
- So I am able, once a month,
I do get a chunk of money,

00:16:41.115 --> 00:16:41.948
and I'm able to hold onto that

00:16:41.948 --> 00:16:44.090
for some time, through the month.

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:44.923
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:16:44.923 --> 00:16:47.690
- Sometimes we get hotel rooms,
but nothing really steady.

00:16:47.690 --> 00:16:48.690
It's just kind of sporadic.

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:52.520
We just kind of take it
by, take it day by day.

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:55.070
Last night, we stayed in a
staircase, in a warm staircase

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:57.580
in this building, this loft
building we know the code for.

00:16:57.580 --> 00:16:59.750
We went in and it was
carpeted and nice and warm

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:01.150
and nobody bugged us.

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:02.190
We were able to sleep through the night

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.610
and left in the morning, left
it clean and no harm, no foul.

00:17:05.610 --> 00:17:06.443
- [Mark] That's great.

00:17:06.443 --> 00:17:07.541
- So.

00:17:07.541 --> 00:17:10.570
- [Mark] That's right, but I
talked to your wife last week

00:17:10.570 --> 00:17:13.640
and you guys both seem like
a great couple. (chuckles)

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:16.200
- Yeah, we're like normal
people, we're just, yeah,

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:17.033
we're just homeless drug addicts,

00:17:17.033 --> 00:17:18.790
kind of this little side thing.

00:17:18.790 --> 00:17:21.980
It's like, oh, by the way,
we're homeless drug addicts.

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:23.130
- [Mark] Yeah, you both seem competent

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:24.530
and intelligent and...

00:17:24.530 --> 00:17:26.780
- Yeah, capable, all of that.

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:28.950
Capable of having normal
lives, we just choose

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:30.780
to have this lifestyle for now.

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:32.440
- [Mark] What is, I mean...

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:33.870
- I don't know what it is.

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:34.703
- [Mark] What is it that
people don't understand

00:17:34.703 --> 00:17:35.536
about addiction, drug addiction?

00:17:35.536 --> 00:17:38.640
- I just, for me, I don't
really make any excuses for it.

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:41.330
I like getting high, and
with this type of drug,

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:43.160
getting high with this type of drug,

00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:44.920
unfortunately, you have to keep it up.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:48.530
You can't just use it and
then take some time off.

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:53.040
You have to stay at it, so
it's like an investment of time

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:56.380
and everything and,
but what it is is just,

00:17:56.380 --> 00:17:57.280
for me, it's a choice.

00:17:57.280 --> 00:18:00.080
I like getting high, I chose
to get high on that drug,

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:01.960
I choose to continue to
get high on that drug

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:05.040
and unless I take some
time and actually detox,

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:07.860
I know what it really takes
to get off of that drug.

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:09.530
I can't just snap my
fingers and get off of it.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:12.516
It would take time and
setting some time aside

00:18:12.516 --> 00:18:17.116
and having a place and the
ability to detox to do that.

00:18:17.116 --> 00:18:17.949
I do it when I'm in jail.

00:18:17.949 --> 00:18:19.430
That's pretty much a lot
of us, that's what we do.

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:20.263
We detox when we're--

00:18:20.263 --> 00:18:21.910
- [Mark] Yeah, yeah, jail's
the way to get clean.

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:24.340
- Get clean when we're in
jail and then some of us say

00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:26.567
we're gonna stay clean when
we get out, some of us don't,

00:18:26.567 --> 00:18:28.365
but it's rare that we do.

00:18:28.365 --> 00:18:30.220
- [Mark] But that's a
common way people get clean.

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:31.543
- Yeah, yeah.

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:34.850
Probably the only clean time
over the last four years

00:18:34.850 --> 00:18:36.350
I've had was the time I was in jail.

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:40.110
- [Mark] Yeah, no, I mean,
it's, I think that the draw

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:44.070
that drugs like heroin
and crack have on people

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.010
is just so strong that...

00:18:46.010 --> 00:18:48.040
- Yeah, it's really a commitment.

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:50.030
It's sort of just like
you almost accepted it.

00:18:50.030 --> 00:18:52.160
It's like, sort of like,
okay, well, here we go again.

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:54.510
You know all of the shit
that comes along with it.

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:56.210
It's not even so much the doing the drug

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:57.043
and the drug itself.

00:18:57.043 --> 00:18:58.890
It's all of the other stuff
that comes along with it,

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:02.750
the making money, having money,
who's gonna have the drug,

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:04.230
who's gonna buy the stuff
that I'm gonna steal

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:05.800
from the store to get the drug.

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:07.810
It's all of that other shit,
all those other relationships,

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:11.670
all that stuff that
becomes time-consuming.

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:14.880
It takes up all, everything,
it takes up your whole time,

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:16.220
your whole day, your whole night.

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:17.660
It becomes everything, becomes your life

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:19.560
and it becomes a lifestyle.

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:21.800
- [Mark] And then there's all
the stigma that goes with it.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:23.260
- Right, and then there's
the judgment of people

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:25.330
and stuff like that and you
sort of just become this,

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:27.060
I don't even really
think about that so much

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:29.360
because, like you said,
when you first look at me,

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:30.870
when a lot of people look at me,

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:33.660
I'm able to keep my
image up and keep myself.

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:35.333
I can get cleaned up, I can
put on nice clothes, I can--

00:19:35.333 --> 00:19:36.880
- [Mark] No, you could totally pass for...

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:37.713
- Cover it up.

00:19:37.713 --> 00:19:38.546
- [Mark] Straight as an arrow.

00:19:38.546 --> 00:19:39.493
- Exactly, so.

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:43.930
It's one of the things,
unlike crack or alcohol

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:45.390
or something like that,
heroin is one of the things

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:47.530
you can definitely keep
hidden if you choose to.

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:49.160
- [Mark] Is it, do you
think it's pretty common?

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:50.870
I asked your wife this.

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:54.010
Is it pretty common?

00:19:54.010 --> 00:19:56.840
Outside of Skid Row, are there people

00:19:56.840 --> 00:19:59.210
using heroin and fentanyl?

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:01.670
- Yeah, yeah, there's
a, the amount of people

00:20:01.670 --> 00:20:04.530
that are downtown is a
small, very small percentage

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:06.870
of the actual drug users
that use that drug.

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:10.770
Most of them are out other
places, Hollywood, the Valley,

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:12.939
places like that, the Westside.

00:20:12.939 --> 00:20:15.178
- [Mark] And they're
trying to function as?

00:20:15.178 --> 00:20:17.300
- Yeah, just trying, yeah,
just still doing the deal.

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:19.850
Santa Monica has a huge, has
a big homeless population,

00:20:19.850 --> 00:20:21.640
and Hollywood does, too.

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:24.242
There's a lot of people that
have just kind of dropped out.

00:20:24.242 --> 00:20:25.390
- [Mark] But do people have
jobs and still use these drugs?

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:26.690
- Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:29.200
I've been a commodities
broker, I've done sales,

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:31.210
and there was a guy who
used to sit behind me

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:34.140
and he would nod out every day,
and see the fentanyl patches

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:36.040
stuck to his, (chuckles)
stuck to his chest,

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:39.200
and this was 2010, 2011,
something like that.

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:40.930
I didn't even use fentanyl
or even heard of it yet

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:42.540
at the time but people were
already starting to use it

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:43.960
back then, 10 years ago.

00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:46.620
It's been about 10 years, it's
been popular about 10 years.

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:50.430
It's really gotten, people
starting to use it a lot more.

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:52.943
- [Mark] Excellent, well, Adam,

00:20:52.943 --> 00:20:53.950
thank you so much for talking to me.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:55.590
- Absolutely, no problem,
it's a pleasure, Mark.

00:20:55.590 --> 00:20:56.423
- [Mark] Very, very interesting.

00:20:56.423 --> 00:20:57.720
- All right, anytime.

00:20:57.720 --> 00:20:58.553
- [Mark] And I wish you the best of luck.

00:20:58.553 --> 00:20:59.386
- You want to continue it again sometime,

00:20:59.386 --> 00:21:00.219
more than welcome to, so.

00:21:00.219 --> 00:21:01.410
- [Mark] Yeah, maybe I'll
have you guys in together.

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:02.964
- Yeah, maybe we'll do
a couples thing, sure.

00:21:02.964 --> 00:21:03.797
- [Mark] That'd be interesting.

00:21:03.797 --> 00:21:04.630
- All right, thanks, Mark.

00:21:04.630 --> 00:21:05.463
- [Mark] Cool, thank you.

